1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,025
A Typical Day for Singapore's Silver Generation

2
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:15,125
For many Singaporeans now in their 80s, the day starts early — not out of habit, but because that's when the shuttle to the nearest Active Ageing Centre picks up.

3
00:00:15,125 --> 00:00:23,500
A quick blood-pressure check, a subsidised breakfast, maybe a round of Rummy-O or a stretching class before the heat sets in.

4
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:30,550
Some still work a few hours a day, clearing tables or wiping counters at the neighbourhood hawker centre.

5
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:42,525
It's an unremarkable morning, and that's rather the point: for a generation that spent decades without much of a safety net, an unremarkable morning is a small daily win.

6
00:00:42,525 --> 00:00:51,250
The reason this generation leans so heavily on subsidised programmes traces back to how they spent their working years.

7
00:00:51,250 --> 00:01:01,850
Many held informal jobs — hawking, portering, piecework — in an economy that hadn't yet built the retirement infrastructure people take for granted now.

8
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:12,975
The Central Provident Fund was set up in 1955 as a compulsory savings scheme, but it took decades to mature into a genuine retirement system.

9
00:01:12,975 --> 00:01:20,400
Someone already well into their working life when it did had little time left to build up savings through it.

10
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:31,450
Large families were, for a long time, the actual retirement plan — and that has become far less reliable as households have shrunk and children have moved out.

11
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:38,850
The government's response has been to build the infrastructure this generation missed the first time around.

12
00:01:38,850 --> 00:01:59,550
The Silver Support Scheme, introduced in 2016, gives seniors with low lifetime wages and little family support a quarterly cash top-up — enhanced in 2025 to as much as $1,080 a quarter, and reaching roughly 260,000 seniors in 2023 alone.

13
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:20,425
Alongside the cash support sit the physical spaces: Active Ageing Centres in almost every neighbourhood, and larger integrated developments like Our Tampines Hub, which opened in 2017 and folds a library, hawker centre, sports facilities, and community programming for seniors into a single town square.

14
00:02:20,425 --> 00:02:41,875
Why it matters today: the generation now in their 80s and 90s is, in many ways, the last to have lived most of their working life before Singapore's retirement systems caught up — which makes the hubs, subsidies, and quiet daily routines built around them one of the more overlooked ways the island is still adjusting for its own history.
